  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1103 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nIf you were outside for any noticeable period this evening, then\nyou know that the much awaited cold front has cleared the region.\nConditions feel much less humid outside, with temperatures\nspanning the 70s for much of the area. Behind this frontal\nboundary, a much cooler and drier airmass will continue to filter\ninto the area on Sunday. High temperatures on Sunday are expected\nto be in the low to mid 80s, which is well below normal for the\nbeginning of August. From an upper-level perspective, the parent\ntrough that drove Saturday's cold frontal boundary will remain\nover the eastern U.S. through Tuesday. This will allow for some\nscattered chances for showers and thunderstorms, mainly in the\nafternoon on Sunday for locations east of the MS River. Coverage\nof any shower and thunderstorm activity Monday and Tuesday will\nlikely be confined to along and east of the TN River. We will\nbegin to see a slow return in the warmer and more humid\ntemperatures Monday and Tuesday, with highs spanning the 80s\nMonday and returning to the upper 80s to low 90s Tuesday.\n\nBy the middle of next week, somewhat zonal flow aloft will begin\nto develop across the Mid-South with a deepening trough axis\nacross the northern Plains. With a developing surface high to the\neast, surface winds will return to a southwesterly flow regime\nand allow an even warmer and more moist airmass to return. This\nwill spell the return of a somewhat typical, summertime pattern\nfor the region, with high temperatures generally in the low to\nmid 90s. Heat index values may begin to approach 105 degrees,\nespecially across the Delta, so some Heat Headlines may be needed\nby the end of the week. As far as precipitation chances go, given\nthe flow regime, latest thoughts are that PoPs will remain fairly\nisolated through the end of the period.",
